## Why This Is Asked
This question exams diagnosing subtle cross-region telemetry issues tied to time synchronization, control-plane stability, MTU handling, and NIC behavior, not just data-path faults.

## Key Concepts
- Time synchronization protocols (NTP/PTP)
- EVPN route convergence and BFD timers
- UDP telemetry MTU/fragmentation implications
- NIC offloads and timestamp behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick log parser for interval spikes
const fs=require('fs');
const lines=fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2],'utf8').split('\n');
let spikes=0;
lines.forEach(l=>{ if(/telemetry.*tail/.test(l)) spikes++;});
console.log('spikes',spikes);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate this diagnostic with a runbook?
- What thresholds would trigger configuration changes (timers, MTU, pacing)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to diagnose cross-region telemetry issues in a service mesh with TLS, tenancy boundaries, and UDP paths.

## Key Concepts
- Edge load balancers TLS session resumption; per-tenant sidecar resource usage; MTU discovery and path fragmentation;
- NAT64 state handling; cross-region tunnel dynamics; observability across L4-L7 boundaries.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick script outline to surface TLS ticket counts from edge LB logs
const fs = require('fs');
const logs = fs.readFileSync('/var/log/edge/tls.log','utf8');
const tickets = logs.match(/TLS_ticket:(\d+)/g) || [];
console.log('Tickets seen:', tickets.length);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely roll out TLS ticket lifetime changes across regions?
- Which metrics differentiate sidecar starvation from path MTU fragmentation in this scenario?
